The rewrite is on the list as it should be. I think your notmuch is
still reading its old database, and that the directory you moved was
not the one it uses.

With the config from dotfiles/notmuch, mail_root is set and
database.path is not. notmuch-config(1), under "DATABASE LOCATION",
then puts the database in $XDG_DATA_HOME/notmuch/default, which is
~/.local/share/notmuch/default. It does not use a .notmuch directory
in the maildir. Here notmuch config get database.path prints
/home/claude/.local/share/notmuch/default, and that directory holds
only xapian/. mail.jj's .gitignore still lists maildir/.notmuch, so
an older database may be left over there on beastie. Moving it does
not affect the database notmuch actually opens.

Two things point to this. First, your view still shows flagged,
archive, and draft. Those are tags you set by hand, so a database
rebuilt without notmuch restore would have lost them. Second, I can
reproduce your view exactly. I checked out mail@upstream (677f5d133ca3)
and the commit before the migration into two scratch maildirs:

- A database built fresh from mail@upstream has 105 threads. The
  signoz port is one 42-message thread, from v1 down to v10.
- A database built before the migration, then updated with notmuch
  new after the files changed, has 149 threads. signoz is 11
  threads, sized 12, 4, 2, 2, 4, 4, 3, 5, 1 and 5 for v1 to v10, the
  same counts as in your view. You have 148 because your copy
  predates my reply.

notmuch new skips a file whose name it has already seen, so
rewriting headers in place never changes threading in an existing
database. That is why it has to be rebuilt.

To rebuild the one notmuch actually uses, keeping your tags:

    db="$(notmuch config get database.path)"
    notmuch dump > /tmp/notmuch.tags
    mv "${db}/xapian" "${db}/xapian.old"
    notmuch new
    notmuch restore < /tmp/notmuch.tags

notmuch new creates the database again when xapian/ is missing. I
tested that with a scratch XDG_DATA_HOME over the current list, and
it came out at 105 threads. Afterwards, notmuch count --output=threads
'*' should print about 105, and the signoz search should show one
thread. Once that checks out, you can delete xapian.old and any
leftover maildir/.notmuch.

My earlier rebuild instructions wrote "<database path>/xapian" without
saying where that is. I should have given the notmuch config command
above instead.
